    "id": "prop:representative-nonoracle",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "At \\(d=1\\), \\(m=6\\), \\(s=4\\), and \\(\\alpha=\\beta=27/200\\), the interaction envelope gives \\(R_n\\gtrsim n^{-243/697}\\); moreover \\(243/697<27/77<5/14\\), so this legal subfamily is slower than both the scalar smooth-functional benchmark \\(n^{-27/77}\\) and the oracle \\(W_1\\) benchmark \\(n^{-5/14}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "d",
      "m",
      "s",
      "\\alpha",
      "\\beta",
      "R_n(d,m,\\alpha,\\beta,s,L)",
      "r_{\\mathrm{or},n}(m,s)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:interaction-lower-envelope",
      "def:oracle-rate"
    ],
    "justification": "A concrete strict exponent separation prevents the proposal from collapsing to a generic maximum of two published rates.",
    "gap": "RobinsEtAl2017HOIF supplies the scalar exponent and NilesWeedBerthet2022 supplies the oracle exponent, but neither likelihood construction contains the shared propensity across a multivariate outcome packing.",
    "consumer": "This tuple is an early known-design benchmark for testing finite-order correction code before solving the full asymmetric phase diagram.",
    "proof_tex": "Use the feasible choices, up to fixed sufficiently small amplitude constants,\n\\[\n h=n^{-800/697},\\qquad \\delta=n^{-27/697},\\qquad\n a=h^{27/200}=n^{-108/697},\\qquad\n b=h^{27/200}=\\delta^4=n^{-108/697}.\n\\]\nThey obey \\(nh=n^{-103/697}\\leq c_L\\) for all sufficiently large \\(n\\).  Moreover,\n\\[\n n^2h\\delta^6b^2(a^2+b^2)\n \\asymp n^{,2-(800+162+432)/697}=1,\n\\]\nand the fixed amplitude constants make this quantity at most \\(c_L\\).  Hence \\(\\texttt{thm:interaction-lower-envelope}\\) and the definition of the envelope give\n\\[\n R_n\\gtrsim \\delta ab=n^{-(27+108+108)/697}=n^{-243/697};\n\\]\nthe exponentially small subtraction is negligible.  Finally,\n\\[\n {27\\over77}-{243\\over697}={108\\over53669}>0,\n \\qquad\n {5\\over14}-{243\\over697}={83\\over9758}>0.\n\\]\nSince \\(r_{\\mathrm{or},n}(6,4)=n^{-5/14}\\) by \\(\\texttt{def:oracle-rate}\\), these identities prove every asserted strict exponent comparison.",
